  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows three main synoptic-scale features this \nafternoon: a nor'easter along the Mid-Atlantic and northeast US, \nHurricane Polo off Baja California, and a longwave trough moving \ninto the western US. The forecast area resides in the middle of \nthese features with weak mid-level energy still meandering around \nthe Mississippi Valley. Shortwave ridging moving overhead favors dry \nconditions, although some weak ascent could generate isolated \nshowers or a storm through the evening. Similar conditions are \nexpected on Monday; some patchy fog is possible overnight into \nMonday morning with a low chance for isolated showers through the \ndaytime hours. Highs reach the 70s to near 80s degrees today and \nMonday. \n\nAttention then turns to the western trough as it advances across the \nDesert Southwest and into the Plains Tuesday through Friday, picking \nup remnants of Polo as it does so. Guidance remains consistent in \nanomalously high PWAT values and a couple rounds of stronger ascent \nthrough this timeframe. Still seeing a slower progression and \nfarther northwest trend with the first wave, pushing the heaviest \nrainfall from southwest Kansas into northeast Nebraska. Portions of \ncentral and north central Kansas could still receive an inch or two \nfrom this first round through Wednesday morning, although a slower \ntrend could limit rainfall with this round. Better forcing arrives \nWednesday through Thursday as the closed upper low and surface cold \nfront move through. Showers and storms become more widespread with \nthis round and lead to the majority of rainfall accumulations. QPF \nfrom ensembles has decreased to some degree; ensemble means are now \n1.5-2.25\". The probability of at least 1\" of rain is still 70-90% \nwith a worst-case scenario of 4-5\" still possible in isolated spots. \nThe highest chances for flooding comes Wednesday afternoon through \nThursday, but there remains uncertainty in overall amounts and \nlocation of the heaviest rainfall. Guidance differs in the speed of \nthe front and how quickly precipitation exists the area, but \nconditions should dry out with cooler temperatures as surface high \npressure builds into the area Friday into Saturday.",
